% The name in a `no_matching_overload` reported for a class function's
% equation is spelled as the source spells it.
%
% The old checker builds this one diagnostic in Haskell and renders the
% name through the real inverse of the runtime mangling, unlike the
% overload failures its solver reports, which keep the mangling's
% escapes. The two spellings only ever differ for a name the mangling
% escaped — a non-ASCII one — which is what this function's name is.
%
% (The equation itself fits neither signature: `inc` is declared only
% on `int`, so it is an inconsistency at the rigid `T` of the first
% signature and at the `string` of the second.)
:- module(typecheck_class_unicode_name, []).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

:- function (inc(int) -> int).
inc(X) -> X.

:- class ('café'(T) -> T), ('café'(string) -> string).
'café'(X) -> inc(X).
